    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-3.3.01-13",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A line can be interrupted without losing its overall linear form. Gaps may reflect land access, unsuitable ground or later development, but the cause needs evidence. Conversely, a curved alignment can follow a winding route and still be linear in the geographical sense. Linear does not require a perfectly straight ruler line. The important feature is an elongated arrangement along a shared axis or corridor.",
